    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • OMODA 4 Lands in Paris To Showcase Futuristic Styling, Looks Like a Discount Urus\n2026 Mazda3 and CX-30 Receive Thorough Upgrades in Australia, Including New Grades and Features\n14 photos\nPhoto: Mazda\nThe Japanese automaker's local division in the Land Down Under wants to make the Mazda CX-30 subcompact crossover SUV and the Mazda3 passenger car irresistible for enthusiasts.\nMazda Australia recently decided to make Mazda MX-5's rivals green with envy with the latest updates for the 2026 model year for the iconic sports car, received a five-star ANCAP safety rating for the all-electric Mazda 6e as well as the latest CX-5, made the six-cylinder hybrid diesel and the plug-in hybrid models more affordable across the market, and also confirmed the pricing and specification for the all-new Mazda CX-6e all-electric crossover SUV, so we can say it has been pretty busy in the Land Down Under.\nWell, the work never ends, and now they are announcing the “abundance of meaningful updates applied for 2026” to the “widely popular” Mazda3 and Mazda CX-30 – with the latter headlining the changes with the addition of not one but two new trim levels: CX-30 G25 Evolve SP and CX-30 G25 GT.\nStarting with the 2026 Mazda CX-30 G25 Evolve SP, the new grade builds upon the Evolve model and adds black cloth seats with red stitching that’s also available on the armrest, center console, and dashboard. Outside, the Evolve SP has a sporty focus with gloss-black trim on the 18-inch alloy wheels, exterior mirrors, roof rails, and badges.\nOn the other hand, the new CX-30 G25 GT focuses on a premium atmosphere with a glass sunroof, black leather interior, heated front seats, and a 10-way powered driver’s seat with position memory. Outside, there are 18-inch silver alloy wheels, silver roof rails, body-colored mirrors, and silver badging. Both grades use the G25 Skyactiv-G powertrain and come with advanced keyless entry.\nAll G25-powered CX-30 models now get a standard Vision Technology package featuring a 360-degree View Monitor with See-Through View, Cruising & Traffic Support (CTS), Driver Monitoring, front parking sensors, Front Cross Traffic Alert (FCTA), and Front Cross Traffic Brake (FCTB). It’s also standard on the Mazda3, but only from the Touring grades upward.\nAdditionally, the CX-30 G25 GT SP debuts White Maztex seats with grey synthetic suede inserts, and also comes with the same gloss-black exterior highlights as the Evolve SP. From now on, the CX-30 GT SP and Astina grades also have ventilated seats, added for the first time across the CX-30 model range. Both the CX-30 and Mazda3 now have a standard 10.25-inch Mazda Connect screen, and it comes with wireless Apple CarPlay and Android Auto across the family.\nThe safety department adds Smart Brake Support (SBS) on the Mazda3 and CX-30 – it now detects motorcycles, turn across traffic, and head-on collisions; there’s also intersection collision avoidance assist. The Mazda CX-30 range gets access to the newly introduced Zinc Green Metallic premium paint; Aero Grey Metallic is available for both the CX-30 and Mazda3, while the Ceramic Metallic and Zircon Sand Metallic are biting the proverbial dust.\n“The Mazda3 and CX-30 continue to be important models within our line-up, offering our customers a compelling combination of award-winning design and engaging driving dynamics – and with the latest updates, we’re adding even more value into the equation,” said Jarrod Gieschen, Director of Sales and Marketing at Mazda Australia .\nThe updated Mazda3 and Mazda CX-30 are available to order; deliveries will start before the end of the month, and pricing kicks off at AU$31,910 (MLP) or about $22,401 at the current exchange rate for the Mazda3 sedan and hatchback, while the CX-30 has a starting point of AU$34,760 or over $24k.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2026 Mazda CX-30 2026 Mazda3 Mazda mazda3 cx-30 2026MY pricing Australia\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT This New Dash Cam Looks Like a Smartwatch, Surprisingly Cheap",
